+# JSRF v15 watchdog: retail heap free-list stall
+
+Date: 2026-09-06
+Retail XBE: Jet Set Radio Future, title ID `0x5345000A`
+Retail XBE SHA-256: `fd19055756719893c466302809b433b785ecf5732df0441286f3f605f0f3ef9c`
+
+## What the v15 Windows trace proves
+
+The v15 run reaches the real retail allocator `sub_001497DC` from `0x0017C926`
+with a 0x10-byte request.  The allocator then calls the Xbox critical-section
+entry thunk at retail `0x00149828`.  The kernel trace reports a successful
+`take 0x00F80610` and immediately returns to guest code.
+
+The 60-second watchdog fires after that successful lock acquisition.  There is
+no matching `drop 0x00F80610` for this final allocator invocation and no
+`[TRACE] <- sub_001497DC` line.
+
+This rules out the earlier working hypothesis that the watchdog was simply
+waiting inside `EnterCriticalSection`: the critical-section call completed.
+Execution is spending the watchdog interval later in the allocator while the
+heap lock is still held.
+
+## Retail allocator mapping
+
+Direct disassembly of the exact retail XBE maps the allocator's large free-list
+logic as follows:
+
+- `0x00149828` - enter the heap critical section
+- `0x00149832` - first instruction after successful lock acquisition
+- `0x001499B3` - begin the large-free-list search
+- `0x00149A1D` - unlink the selected large free block
+- `0x00149B1A` - first reinsertion path
+- `0x00149B28` - first sorted large-list walk loop
+- `0x00149C09` - second sorted large-list walk loop
+- `0x00149D5B` - third sorted large-list walk loop
+- `0x00149DD8` - critical-section leave path
+- `0x00149DDE` / `0x00149F45` - normal allocator return path / final epilogue
+
+The watchdog register sample is consistent with one of the sorted large-list
+walks: `ebx=0x00F80000` is the heap base, `ecx=0x00F80180` is exactly the
+large-list sentinel (`heap + 0x180`), and the live `eax/edi/esi/edx` values have
+the shape of the node pointer, node header and remainder-size values used by
+those loops.
+
+That makes a malformed/cyclic large free list the strongest current hypothesis,
+but v15 does not contain enough live link data to prove which loop is repeating
+or which node first becomes malformed.  Do not replace the allocator or change
+synchronization semantics on this evidence alone.
+
+## v16 diagnostic
+
+v16 adds a diagnostic-only generated-code probe at the seven exact retail
+landmarks above.  `jsrf_heap_probe()` records:
+
+- probe VA and hit count;
+- guest ESP and general registers;
+- current list node address;
+- node `next` / `prev` links and size units;
+- large-list sentinel address and its `next` / `prev` links.
+
+The callback only reads guest state.  It does not modify guest registers, heap
+memory, critical-section state, allocator branching, input, rendering, or game
+logic.  Logging is throttled after the first few hits so an actual loop remains
+observable without filling the log indefinitely.
+
+## What the next Windows run will distinguish
+
+- Repeated `HEAPPROBE` hits at one loop VA with the same node/link tuple proves a
+  self-loop or stationary walk.
+- Alternating/repeating node tuples prove a short cycle and identify its exact
+  members.
+- Monotonically changing nodes with no repetition points away from list
+  corruption and toward a different long-running allocator path.
+- If the probe reaches `0x00149DD8`, the allocator itself escaped and the stall
+  is downstream instead.
+
+No gameplay implementation is changed by this diagnostic.
